despite being devastated by a powerful  earthquake in 2013 these century-old  churches remained a top tourist  destination until the pandemic broke out  the months-long lockdown and the closing  of international borders to visitors  devastated the tourism industry here for  months leaving thousands unemployed  just as the visayas province was slowly  recovering last year typhoon ride  destroyed many parts in december  who works on a tourist boat says the  lack of visitors was just too much  it was like you were happy the other day  and the next day you're sad because you  might not have a job the next day  how could they pay my expenses so when  we started operating again even though  we only had two customers it felt like  we were already back  people say visiting behalf is synonymous  with seeing the entire country the  province is rich with biodiversity and  steep in history this is why it's long  been called the heart of the philippine  islands  the tourism industry accounts for nearly  12 percent of gdp so the government's  decision to reopen the country to  international travelers again as kovid  cases decrease significantly is giving  many here a renewed sense of hope and  purpose  the impact is so positive because you're  talking about the drivers the porters  the the tour operators we're looking at  a great multiplier effect and that also  reverberates to agriculture that  reverberates to fisheries because all of  that influx of visitors contributes to a  volume of need  earthquakes a super typhoon and the  pandemic the litany of tragedies may  seem unbearable  but the roman catholic church has always  served as a moral and spiritual compass  for communities here  and so in times of hardship the faithful  say prayers to god and know that somehow  there will be a better way  jamilah linda al-jazeera bohol province  central philippines
 

